---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../site';
---

<BaseLayout title="about" current="about">
  <article class="prose">
    <figure class="crest">
      <img src="/emblem.png" width="360" height="360" alt={`The ${site.name} maker's seal: a bearded pirate skull in a ship's wheel, over crossed cutlasses, above an "x-hakt" banner.`} />
    </figure>

    <h1 style="font-size:1.6rem;letter-spacing:-0.02em;margin-bottom:1rem">about</h1>

    <p>
      A captain with no ship and no crew keeps a log anyway. That is this site.
      I build and run a small fleet of projects at home, mostly alongside an AI
      agent, spread across an old laptop, a rented box or two, and whatever I am
      typing on. When I work out how a piece of it should be wired, I write it
      down — partly for whoever comes next, mostly for the version of me who
      will have forgotten by spring.
    </p>

    <p>
      So the notes are a practitioner describing their own setup, one diagram
      per idea. If you came here looking for <em>how</em> rather than
      <em>whether</em>, you are in the right place. Entries are signed
      <code>-x</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>The pirate streak</h2>
    <p>
      It is a maker's mark and a turn of phrase, nothing more. The seal above is
      real artwork; a few corners of the interface borrow a word from the sea
      (the map, the spyglass). The filing itself is plain: notes are grouped by
      the kind of work they are, and tagged with the technology they use. If a
      nautical label ever gets in the way of finding something, that is a bug.
    </p>

    <h2>The wheelhouse</h2>
    <p>
      The setup these notes describe is run day to day from
      <a href="/locker/bosun-x/">bosun-x</a> — a dashboard for the projects and
      the machines they sit on, built out of the same plain files it watches
      over. It is open source. This site is the logbook; bosun-x is the wheel.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .crest {
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
    text-align: center;
  }
  .crest img {
    width: 260px;
    height: auto;
    max-width: 60%;
  }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .crest img { width: 180px; }
  }
</style>
